You start with a noisy 3D point cloud.
Instead of treating points independently or using fixed k-NN neighborhoods, you:

👉 Build a Delaunay triangulation (DT)
👉 Use its tetrahedra and facets as a graph
👉 Learn how noise behaves using this geometry-aware structure
👉 Predict small corrections (offsets) to each point

The network does not generate new points — it shifts noisy points slightly to cleaner positions.

Step-by-step explanation
1️⃣ Input: Noisy Point Cloud + DT Graph

What comes in

A noisy point cloud with N points

Each point has 6D features (typically XYZ + normals / curvature / intensity)

A Delaunay triangulation built over the point set:

Tetrahedra → volumetric neighborhoods

Facets (triangles) → shared boundaries between tetrahedra

Why DT?

DT adapts to point density

Encodes true geometric neighbors

Naturally captures edges, corners, and surface discontinuities

2️⃣ Point Feature Extraction (MLP)

What happens

Each point is processed independently by a shared MLP:

6D → 64D → 128D


Output: a 128D feature vector per point

Why

Converts raw coordinates/features into a learnable embedding

Still local — no neighborhood mixing yet

Same MLP is used for all points (weight sharing)

3️⃣ Cell (Tetrahedron) Feature Extraction

Each tetrahedron has 4 vertices.

What happens

Collect the 128D features of its 4 points

Aggregate them (mean / max / concat)

Add geometric properties of the tetrahedron:

volume

edge lengths

aspect ratio

centroid

Then pass through another MLP:

(point features + geometry) → 128D cell feature


Why

Noise patterns are easier to understand in local volumes

Tetrahedra encode local surface smoothness vs irregularity

Gives the network structure-aware context

4️⃣ Graph Convolution on DT Cells (×3 layers)

Now tetrahedra are treated as nodes in a graph.

Edges

Two tetrahedra are neighbors if they share a facet

What happens

Graph convolution / message passing:

Each cell exchanges information with adjacent cells

Repeated 3 times to expand context

Why

Allows information flow across the surface

Helps distinguish:

real sharp features (consistent across neighbors)

noise (random, inconsistent)

This is where geometric deep learning truly happens.

5️⃣ Facet-Level Processing (Edge Awareness)

Each facet (triangle) lies between two tetrahedra.

What happens

Combine features from the two neighboring cells

Learn a facet representation

Why

Sharp edges and corners lie along facets

Facets capture discontinuities

Helps prevent over-smoothing across edges

This is crucial for feature-preserving denoising.

6️⃣ Point Offset Prediction

Now information flows back to points.

What happens

Each point receives refined features from cells/facets

Final MLP predicts a 3D offset:

128D → 64D → (Δx, Δy, Δz)


Why offsets instead of new points?

Residual learning is more stable

Small corrections preserve topology

Common in high-quality denoising networks

7️⃣ Final Output
Denoised Point = Noisy Point + Predicted Offset


Result

Noise is reduced

Sharp features are preserved

Point count and structure remain unchanged

Why this approach is powerful

✔ Uses true geometric neighborhoods (DT)
✔ Separates volume reasoning (cells) and boundary reasoning (facets)
✔ Scales to large point clouds
✔ Naturally handles high noise
✔ Feature-preserving by design